1. Document Purpose
This document defines the requirements for the redesigned Dark Fusion Reactor experience and the Luasquare framework capabilities required to implement it.
The requirements are intended to guide development of:
1.	the reusable Luasquare framework;
2.	the Dark Fusion Reactor Lua package included within Luasquare;
3.	the Dark Fusion Reactor Hammer map and supporting assets;
4.	the integration between Lua-owned simulation and Hammer-placed Source entities.
The document shall be used as a foundation for:
•	framework development;
•	reactor simulation design;
•	Hammer map modification;
•	module implementation;
•	interface design;
•	integration testing;
•	public documentation;
•	Workshop release planning.
This is a living document. Some reactor mechanics, mathematical relationships, timing values, targetnames, and audiovisual sequences remain intentionally undefined and will be expanded during later design discussions.
________________________________________
2. Product Definition
2.1 Product Identity
Dark Fusion Reactor is an advanced scripted Garry’s Mod map centered on operating a large fictional power reactor.
The redesigned product shall remain faithful to the identity of the original published Workshop map. It shall continue to present itself primarily as:
•	a scripted Garry’s Mod map;
•	a playable reactor-operation experience;
•	a technical and cinematic sandbox;
•	a map whose principal attraction is its reactor system.
The redesigned product shall not become a separate standalone game mode, competitive mode, or conventional campaign.
2.2 Relationship to the Existing Map
The existing published map shall be treated as the Legacy Version.
The Legacy Version is notable because its reactor, sequences, and control logic are implemented almost entirely through Source Engine entity I/O without Lua programming.
The Legacy Version shall remain available separately and should be preserved as an example of advanced Source I/O map scripting.
The redesigned Dark Fusion Reactor shall be released as a separate Workshop product rather than replacing the Legacy Version.
The redesigned product shall:
•	retain the original concept and identity;
•	reuse or reinterpret the existing physical map;
•	preserve recognizable reactor visuals and lore;
•	replace major Source I/O logic with Lua-owned systems;
•	provide substantially more detailed operation;
•	provide more complex failure and recovery behavior;
•	use Luasquare as a required dependency.
2.3 Intended Experience
The reactor shall feel like an extremely large and complex fictional industrial machine.
The experience shall combine:
•	science-fiction concepts;
•	internally consistent system behavior;
•	selected realistic power-plant concepts;
•	operator procedures;
•	resource management;
•	control-room instrumentation;
•	audiovisual spectacle;
•	catastrophic event sequences.
The science does not need to represent real physics accurately.
However, the reactor shall behave according to defined internal rules. A player who studies those rules should be able to predict system responses, improve their operation, and understand why failures occurred.
2.4 Intended Player Reaction
A new player is expected to feel overwhelmed when first encountering the redesigned reactor.
This is an accepted and partially intentional quality of the experience.
The system shall not be simplified solely to ensure that an unprepared player can immediately operate it successfully.
Complexity should come from interacting systems and procedures rather than from deliberately concealed or inconsistent rules.
2.5 Learning Model
The redesigned map shall not require a fully guided in-game tutorial.
The primary learning materials may include:
•	an external written manual;
•	Steam Workshop documentation;
•	an instructional YouTube video;
•	readable notes placed inside the map;
•	control labels;
•	diagrams;
•	DECAOS warnings and operational feedback.
DECAOS may provide situational guidance, but shall not function as a complete step-by-step tutorial during normal gameplay.
The map should allow players to learn through:
•	observation;
•	experimentation;
•	failure;
•	external documentation;
•	repeated operation.
