4. Core Gameplay Model
4.1 Primary Activity
Reactor operation shall be the central activity and main selling point of the map.
Other environmental, exploratory, or cinematic elements may support the experience but shall not replace the reactor as the primary focus.
4.2 Session Structure
A typical session may contain:
1.	map exploration and preparation;
2.	fuel and subsystem inspection;
3.	startup authorization;
4.	automatic or manual startup;
5.	annihilation-stage operation;
6.	dark-fusion ignition;
7.	normal power operation;
8.	power-quota management;
9.	subsystem maintenance and fuel production;
10.	controlled shutdown, emergency shutdown, or disaster;
11.	post-ending exploration of the altered environment.
4.3 Session Duration
The Legacy Version typically supports sessions of approximately two to three hours from startup to a meltdown ending.
The redesigned map does not yet have a fixed target session duration.
The redesigned simulation may produce longer or shorter sessions depending on:
•	startup method;
•	operator skill;
•	selected operating power;
•	quota behavior;
•	random failures;
•	fuel-production demands;
•	shutdown decisions;
•	disaster progression.
Session duration shall be evaluated during playtesting rather than fixed during the initial requirements phase.
4.4 Success and Failure
The reactor shall not have a conventional victory condition.
The operator may continue operation until they choose to shut down or until the reactor reaches a terminal condition.
Performance shall be evaluated primarily through an operator score.
The score is expected to represent the financial value of exported electricity, potentially using a fictional future-market value expressed in currency per megawatt-hour.
A conceptual form is:
Operator Revenue
    = Credited Exported Energy
    × Market Value per MWh
    - Safety Penalties
    - Equipment Damage Costs
    - Internal Consumption Costs
    - Failure Penalties
The final scoring formula remains to be defined.
The player’s practical goals shall be to:
•	export as much credited energy as possible;
•	satisfy power quotas;
•	avoid unsafe overproduction;
•	minimize equipment damage;
•	minimize unnecessary internal consumption;
•	avoid catastrophic failure;
•	complete a controlled shutdown when desired.
4.5 Terminal Conditions
Terminal conditions may include:
•	successful but costly Reactor Purge;
•	unrecoverable runaway fusion;
•	singularity containment breach;
•	other future disaster endings.
A terminal condition shall end meaningful reactor operation but does not need to force the player out of the map.
________________________________________
5. Single-Player and Multiplayer Direction
5.1 Original Design
The original map was designed primarily as a single-player experience.
The redesigned reactor shall remain fully operable by one player.
No required procedure shall permanently depend on the presence of multiple players unless a separate multiplayer-specific mode is introduced later.
5.2 Multiplayer Support
The redesigned map should support cooperative multiplayer because of:
•	player demand expressed through Workshop comments;
•	the benefits of dividing complex operator tasks;
•	the current server-authoritative design of Luasquare;
•	the potential for different players to operate different subsystems.
Multiplayer support does not initially require formal operator roles.
Players may be allowed to interact with any available control unless future design introduces permissions.
5.3 Control Arbitration
When multiple players attempt to use the same control:
•	the server shall validate the interaction;
•	the first valid interaction shall be accepted;
•	the control shall enter a short interaction or movement lock;
•	later conflicting interactions shall be ignored until the lock clears.
This behavior shall prevent simultaneous use from producing invalid control states.
Advanced role ownership, permissions, or operator stations are not required for the first implementation.
