8. Luasquare Framework Definition
8.1 Current Definition
Luasquare is a Garry’s Mod Lua map-simulation framework.
It connects Hammer-placed Source entities to Lua-owned simulation systems.
Hammer provides:
•	physical map layout;
•	geometry;
•	props;
•	moving entities;
•	buttons;
•	levers;
•	lights;
•	sprites;
•	display anchors;
•	relays;
•	targetnames;
•	audiovisual entities.
Lua provides:
•	simulation state;
•	update loops;
•	control logic;
•	plant systems;
•	instrumentation;
•	alarms;
•	map entity commands;
•	networking;
•	debugging;
•	map-specific orchestration.
8.2 Framework Character
Luasquare is not currently:
•	a visual scripting system;
•	a Hammer extension;
•	a node editor;
•	a custom entity-component framework;
•	a typed wiring environment;
•	a custom map editor.
Luasquare is currently based on:
•	Lua tables and namespaces;
•	registration functions;
•	named module instances;
•	shared state tables;
•	getter callbacks;
•	independent update timers;
•	Garry’s Mod networking;
•	Hammer targetnames;
•	Source entity inputs;
•	map-specific bootstrap files.
8.3 Name Meaning
“Luasquare” is a viable fictional project name.
The word “square” does not currently represent a technical framework object.
Requirements shall not use “square” to mean:
•	a node;
•	a component;
•	a device;
•	a processing block;
•	a map region;
•	a connection.
The preferred technical terms are:
•	framework;
•	module;
•	subsystem;
•	registered instance;
•	display;
•	control;
•	map binding;
•	map target;
•	bootstrap;
•	simulation state.
