13. Targetname Management
13.1 Registration Model
Targetnames may continue to be declared inside the relevant registration block.
Example:
LUASQUARE_SEG7.RegisterDisplay('reactor_export_tw', {
    'dfr_export_tw_0',
    'dfr_export_tw_1',
    'dfr_export_tw_2',
    'dfr_export_tw_3'
})
A separate universal registry is not mandatory for the initial implementation.
13.2 Targetname Redesign
The DFR VMF may rename legacy targetnames.
New targetnames should use a consistent convention.
Suggested prefixes include:
dfr_core_
dfr_stabilizer_
dfr_catalyzer_
dfr_ctrl_
dfr_disp_
dfr_alarm_
dfr_fx_
dfr_audio_
dfr_timeline_
dfr_fuel_
dfr_thermal_
13.3 Targetname Requirements
Targetnames shall:
•	be unique where uniqueness is required;
•	identify the related subsystem;
•	avoid ambiguous abbreviations;
•	remain stable after public release where practical;
•	be documented for Lua integration;
•	distinguish control entities from visual entities.
13.4 Binding Classification
Each required map binding shall be classified as one of:
Critical
A missing binding prevents the reactor from operating safely or meaningfully.
Examples:
•	main reactor movement entity;
•	startup controls;
•	required fuel receptacles;
•	essential state-control relays.
Required Gameplay
A missing binding breaks a major procedure but does not necessarily prevent framework initialization.
Examples:
•	one Catalyzer control;
•	shutdown control;
•	district power switch.
Presentation
A missing binding removes expected audiovisual feedback.
Examples:
•	alarm light;
•	display digit;
•	effect relay;
•	music source.
Optional
A missing binding does not prevent normal reactor operation.
Examples:
•	secondary ambient effect;
•	decorative indicator;
•	nonessential environmental response.
