16. Simulation Timing Model
16.1 Server Authority
The server shall own the authoritative DFR simulation.
Clients shall not independently calculate authoritative reactor behavior.
16.2 Central DFR Update Loop
DFR shall introduce a central simulation update loop.
A conceptual interface is:
DFR.Update(deltaTime)
The central update loop shall coordinate:
•	reactor state transitions;
•	core calculations;
•	annihilation behavior;
•	dark-fusion behavior;
•	Stabilizer behavior;
•	damage progression;
•	thermal behavior;
•	quota and scoring behavior;
•	major failure detection.
16.3 Lag Behavior
The simulation shall prioritize stability and resilience over strict real-time progression.
If the server slows down:
•	the reactor simulation may slow down with it;
•	the system shall avoid extreme single-step calculations;
•	the system shall avoid producing large instant changes from one delayed timer update;
•	safety thresholds shall not be skipped because of an unusually large time step.
Strict deterministic replay is not required.
16.4 Delta-Time Handling
The update loop should:
•	measure elapsed simulation time;
•	clamp unusually large delta values;
•	use stable rate-based calculations;
•	avoid calculations that depend on rendered frame rate;
•	avoid catching up through an unbounded number of update steps.
A possible policy is:
Normal delay:
    Process measured delta time.

Large delay:
    Clamp delta time to a safe maximum.

Severe stall:
    Slow simulation progression rather than attempting a large catch-up.
Exact values remain to be selected through testing.
16.5 Subsystem Update Rates
Not all systems need the same response speed.
The reactor is canonically extremely large. Its supporting infrastructure extends through the spinal superstructure of a Half-Life 2 Citadel and may span kilometers vertically.
Local reactor systems may react relatively quickly.
Remote or physically massive systems may react more slowly.
Examples of slower systems include:
•	coolant pumps on distant levels;
•	secondary thermal plant turbines;
•	large coolant networks;
•	district-scale electrical switching;
•	heat migration;
•	superstructure temperature changes;
•	long-distance mechanical response.
This difference in response time shall contribute to the sense of scale.
________________________________________
17. Timeline and Music Timing
17.1 Simulation Priority
Physical simulation shall take priority over perfect music synchronization.
The reactor shall not be forced into physically invalid states solely to remain synchronized with a soundtrack.
17.2 Sectioned Music
Long music tracks may be separated into multiple files or sections.
The system may crossfade between sections when:
•	a timeline phase changes;
•	startup progresses;
•	a fault interrupts a sequence;
•	the reactor changes state;
•	a disaster escalates.
17.3 Timeline Relationship
The future timeline system shall coordinate presentation with simulation but shall not replace the authoritative reactor simulation.
A timeline may:
•	request a state transition;
•	temporarily restrict controls;
•	trigger audiovisual events;
•	wait for simulation conditions;
•	provide controlled overrides where explicitly defined.
If the simulation is delayed, music transitions may also be delayed or moved to a safe loop point.
The final synchronization policy shall be refined after the timeline engine and music assets exist.
