18. Scale and Response Characteristics
18.1 Reactor Scale
The Dark Fusion Reactor shall be treated as a massive installation.
The reactor’s superstructure is part of a Citadel-scale spinal structure extending for kilometers.
The simulation should communicate this scale through:
•	delayed mechanical responses;
•	long startup processes;
•	distant subsystem activity;
•	large thermal inertia;
•	large stored energy;
•	delayed consequences;
•	communication with remote plant levels;
•	slow response from secondary systems.
18.2 Mixed Response Speeds
Different variables shall have different response characteristics.
Examples:
Fast Response
•	control input recognition;
•	alarm activation;
•	injection-valve command;
•	electrical trip signal;
•	emergency cutoff command.
Moderate Response
•	containment-field change;
•	annihilation equilibrium;
•	director-beam alignment;
•	Catalyzer output;
•	energy saturation.
Slow Response
•	coolant-flow changes across the Citadel;
•	turbine output;
•	bulk temperature;
•	sea-heating effects;
•	superstructure recovery;
•	fuel production;
•	remote plant deployment.
Exact time constants shall be defined during simulation design.
________________________________________
19. Multiplayer State Synchronization
19.1 Authoritative State
The server shall remain authoritative for all DFR module state.
19.2 Client State
Clients shall receive the data required for:
•	3D2D displays;
•	graphs;
•	alarm presentation;
•	DECAOS subtitles;
•	music state;
•	control positions;
•	cinematic effects;
•	debug views.
19.3 Late Joining
A late-joining player should receive:
•	current reactor state;
•	current subsystem values required by displays;
•	current control positions;
•	active alarms;
•	graph history where supported;
•	active timeline phase;
•	current music section;
•	current disaster stage.
The existing 3D2D framework already broadcasts display state and graph history. DFR shall extend this principle to additional presentation systems.
________________________________________
20. Control Interaction Requirements
20.1 Native Hammer Controls
DFR controls shall use native Hammer entities.
Wiremod shall not be required.
Likely control entities include:
•	func_button;
•	func_rot_button;
•	func_movelinear;
•	prop_dynamic;
•	relay-driven control assemblies.
20.2 Logical Control State
Every important control shall have a Lua-owned logical state.
Possible control states include:
•	unavailable;
•	available;
•	moving;
•	enabled;
•	disabled;
•	locked;
•	tripped;
•	malfunctioning;
•	overridden.
20.3 Interaction Lock
A control shall enter a temporary lock after accepting an interaction when required by its physical or logical behavior.
During the lock:
•	conflicting uses shall be rejected;
•	repeated Hammer outputs shall not duplicate the action;
•	the physical animation shall be allowed to complete;
•	the operator should receive feedback if the control remains unavailable.
20.4 State Synchronization
Lua should be able to command the corresponding Hammer entity so that:
•	button position;
•	lever position;
•	animation;
•	lock state;
•	availability;
remain consistent with authoritative logical state.
________________________________________
21. Error Handling Policy
21.1 General Policy
Luasquare and DFR shall fail loudly.
Unexpected framework or simulation errors shall not be silently hidden if they could leave the reactor in an invalid state.
21.2 Critical Error Behavior
When an unexpected critical Lua error occurs:
•	the DFR simulation shall stop;
•	active update loops shall stop or become inert;
•	control interactions shall be disabled where possible;
•	a prominent error shall be printed;
•	the failed module and stack information shall be exposed;
•	the player or developer shall be informed that the map requires reload.
The system shall not attempt to continue operating with potentially corrupted reactor state.
21.3 Presentation Getter Errors
Display or alarm getter functions may remain wrapped in protected calls.
A failed display getter may:
•	show an error state;
•	show unavailable data;
•	print a warning;
•	continue allowing the reactor simulation to run.
A presentation failure shall only halt the entire simulation if it indicates broader state corruption.
21.4 Development Philosophy
During development, detailed failures are preferable to silent fallback behavior.
The first implementation does not require automatic module restart or fault recovery after programming errors.
________________________________________
22. Reset, Cleanup, and Ending Behavior
22.1 Normal Ending Behavior
After a terminal reactor ending, the map shall remain loaded.
The reactor does not need to automatically reset itself.
Players shall be free to:
•	explore the damaged environment;
•	observe remaining effects;
•	inspect the result of the event;
•	continue sandbox activity where possible.
22.2 Recommended Reset
To begin a new complete session, the player shall be encouraged to reload the map.
A full reload is preferred because it:
•	restores all Hammer entities;
•	clears spawned objects;
•	resets Lua state;
•	stops lingering effects;
•	restores map memory state;
•	avoids incomplete manual cleanup.
22.3 In-Session Reset
A complete in-session reactor reset is not required for the first DFR implementation.
A future development reset command may be added for testing.
22.4 Cleanup Compatibility
The current framework is generally not destroyed by Garry’s Mod cleanup commands because core map entities are not normal spawned props.
However, DFR may deploy or spawn additional physical modules and objects.
The final system shall define cleanup behavior for:
•	spawned fuel cells;
•	spawned debris;
•	temporary props;
•	deployed modules;
•	dynamically created effects;
•	debug entities.
If cleanup removes required runtime objects, DFR may:
•	stop the simulation;
•	report that a map reload is required;
•	or rerun selected bootstrap registration where safe.
Full automatic recovery after “Clean Up Everything” is not mandatory for the first public version.
